    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d284">

                <pb facs="0529" n="467" sameAs="frus:frus1944Quebec/pg467"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Roosevelt Papers</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Minute by President Roosevelt and Prime Minister
                        Churchill</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d284-f1"/></head>
                <p>The President and Prime Minister have agreed to put to Marshal Stalin Lord
                    Simon’s proposals for dealing with the major war criminals,<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d284-f2"/> and to concert with him a list of names.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>OK   F[<hi rendition="#small-caps">ranklin</hi>] D R[<hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">oosevelt</hi>]</signed><anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d284-f3"/>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-left">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Quebec</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1944-09-15">15th September, 1944</date>.</dateline>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d284-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d284-f1-content">
                <p>In the Roosevelt Papers this document and the minute relating to the French
                    Committee of National Liberation (<hi rendition="#italic">post</hi>, <ref
                        target="#pg469">p. 469</ref>) are attached to the following memorandum of
                    September 16, 1944, from Churchill’s Assistant Private Secretary to the
                    President’s Naval Aide: “<hi rendition="#small-caps">Admiral Wilson Brown</hi>:
                    I am sending for your records copies of minutes about the Agreement reached by
                    the President and the Prime Minister yesterday on two points, namely, Treatment
                    of War Criminals and the status of the French Committee of National Liberation.
                    J. R. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Colville</hi>.” Roosevelt forwarded copies of
                    the two minutes to Hull for his information on September 21, 1944.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d284-f2" n="2" xml:id="d284-f2-content">
                <p>For the Simon proposals referred to, see <hi rendition="#italic">ante</hi>, <ref
                        target="#pg91">p. 91</ref>. Cf. a draft of a telegram to Stalin dated
                    September 17, 1944, <hi rendition="#italic">post</hi>, <ref target="#pg489">p.
                        489</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d284-f3" n="3" xml:id="d284-f3-content">
                <p>On the source text “OK FDR” appears in Roosevelt’s handwriting. No indication is
                    given that the minute was initialed by Churchill.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
